\title{A Spherical-Cap Power Ceiling for Near-Threshold Correlation Tests}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2 September 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We study the resolving power of test statistics when two competing hypotheses are realised by
predictor vectors that are nearly parallel. Writing $\uh = u/\|u\|$ for the direction of a
coordinate vector $u \in \R^n$ and $\corr(u,v) = \langle u,v\rangle/(\|u\|\|v\|)$, we begin from
the exact chordal identity $\|\uh - \vh\|^2 = 2 - 2\corr(u,v)$, so that an alignment hypothesis
$\corr(u,v) \ge 1-\varepsilon$ confines the entire testing problem to a spherical cap of chordal
radius $\sqrt{2\varepsilon}$ and angular radius at most $(\pi/2)\sqrt{2\varepsilon}$. From this we
derive a sample-size-free power ceiling: every statistic $F$ that is $L$-Lipschitz on the unit
sphere satisfies $|F(\uh)-F(\vh)| \le L\sqrt{2\varepsilon}$, with no dependence on the ambient
dimension. The bound is invariant under $m$-fold replication, because correlation --- and hence
the chord --- is exactly replication-invariant; it is sharp, being attained by a distance
statistic for every $L$; and the constrained class is non-vacuous, since correlation against a
fixed unit response is exactly $1$-Lipschitz on the sphere. Applied to a recorded near-threshold
configuration in which a pooled reading of $0.558$ is compared against a pre-registered floor of
$0.550$, with competing predictors alignable to $0.9999$, the ceiling is $\sqrt2/100 =
0.014142\ldots$ (angular radius $0.81027^\circ < 0.9^\circ$), while the recorded margin
$\delta = 0.008$ forces $\corr(u,v) \le 1-\delta^2/2 = 0.999968$. We identify the optimal smooth
test: correlation against the contrast direction $(\uh-\vh)/\|\uh-\vh\|$ separates the hypotheses
by \emph{exactly} the chordal distance, so the maximum over the sphere-Lipschitz class equals that
distance and is attained by a correlation statistic; at the recorded instance it improves on the
response correlation by at most a factor $1.7678$. A capacity theorem shows the cap admits at most
one resolvable ladder rung at the recorded margin, and that exactly one is attained. A H\"older
generalisation replaces the ceiling by $C(\sqrt{2\varepsilon})^\alpha$ without removing the
obstruction, while a discontinuous rank/threshold statistic attains maximal separation $1$ on the
same configuration and is Lipschitz for no constant --- locating the escape route precisely at the
loss of continuity.
\end{abstract}

