\section{Algorithms}

\textbf{Cap diagnostic.} Given predictors $u,v$ and response $w$: compute $\rho = \corr(u,v)$ in
$O(n)$; report chordal radius $r = \sqrt{2-2\rho}$ and angular radius $\arccos\rho$ (certified
$\le (\pi/2)r$); report the ceiling $Lr$ at sensitivity $L$, the reading gap
$\delta = |\corr(u,w)-\corr(v,w)|$, the required constant $L_{\min} = 1/r$ for full separation, and
the alignment ceiling $1-\delta^2/2$. Steps needing only $u,v$ constitute a \emph{design-stage}
screen: they require no data collected under either hypothesis.

\textbf{Optimal smooth test.} Normalise $u,v$; form $d = \uh-\vh$; if $\|d\| = 0$ report that no
smooth test separates the hypotheses; otherwise set $e = d/\|d\|$ and report the separation
$\langle\uh,e\rangle - \langle\vh,e\rangle = \|d\|$, certified optimal by
Theorem~\ref{thm:optimum}. Cost $O(n)$.

\textbf{Cap capacity.} Given $\varepsilon,\delta,L$, report $k_{\max} = \lfloor
L\sqrt{2\varepsilon}/\delta\rfloor$, the largest number of rungs of a monotone ladder that a stable
statistic can resolve inside the cap.

\section{Applications and scope}

\emph{Design-stage power screening.} The traditional power calculation asks for the $n$ needed to
detect a given effect. Theorem~\ref{thm:ceiling} answers a logically prior question that no $n$ can
override: is the statistic capable of seeing the difference at all? Since the alignment between
hypothesised predictors is computable before data collection, a programme can be screened for
geometric futility in advance. A pre-registration specifying both a smooth statistic and two
hypotheses aligned above $1-\delta^2/2$ is internally inconsistent.

\emph{Diagnosing failed replications.} When a near-threshold result fails to sharpen across many
replications, the usual explanations are publication bias, heterogeneity, or a null effect. Here is
a fourth, testable one: the hypotheses are geometrically indistinguishable at the resolution of the
statistic, and replication is invariant on exactly the quantity that matters. Its signature is that
the observed separation sits near a fixed ceiling that does \emph{not} shrink with pooled sample
size.

\emph{Choosing among statistics.} Theorem~\ref{thm:optimum} makes the trade explicit: the best
smooth test is a projection onto the contrast direction, and its advantage over a reasonable
alternative is bounded --- at (R), by $1.7678$. Any hope of a decisive verdict must come from
outside the smooth class.

\emph{Ladder audits.} Theorem~\ref{thm:capacity} gives an internal-consistency check for programmes
reporting graded ladders: at most $L\sqrt{2\varepsilon}/\delta$ rungs are resolvable inside a cap of
alignment $1-\varepsilon$ at per-rung margin $\delta$.

\emph{Scope.} The results are deterministic geometry, not distribution theory: they bound the
separation of \emph{values} between two fixed hypothesis realisations, not the probability of a
decision error. Sampling noise can only widen the overlap, so the ceilings are optimistic. The
framework applies to any scale-invariant summary (Pearson, Spearman, cosine similarity), and the
Lipschitz hypothesis is needed only on the sphere.

\section{Discussion}

The result has the shape of an information-theoretic impossibility theorem with none of its
machinery: a two-line inequality --- Cauchy--Schwarz plus a Lipschitz hypothesis --- that is exactly
tight, and whose tightness is what makes it useful. It is easy to miss because it lives in the wrong
coordinate system for standard practice: power is parameterised by effect size and sample size,
while the controlling quantity here, the mutual alignment of the hypotheses, is neither.

Three features deserve emphasis. Dimension-freeness is structural, not asymptotic: the resource
required is not of the type that sampling produces. Sharpness converts a bound into a design
principle: since the ceiling is attained by an implementable statistic, one knows both the maximum
available and how to collect it. And the escape route is exactly one hypothesis wide: Lipschitz,
H\"older, chain and ladder consequences all fall to the same argument, so continuity is precisely
the load-bearing hypothesis --- which suggests total variation, not sensitivity, as the correct
currency for the escape.

\section{Future directions}

\emph{A total-variation floor for discontinuous crossing tests.} Escaping the Lipschitz ceiling
costs variation: a statistic separating two directions $\delta$ apart in value while they are
$\sqrt{2\varepsilon}$ apart in distance must concentrate at least $\delta$ of total variation inside
a cap of radius $\sqrt{2\varepsilon}$. With an exact cap radius and an exact attained separation in
hand, the trade-off can be stated as a sharp inequality; the one-dimensional (great-circle) case is
immediately tractable.

\emph{Cap capacity as a packing invariant.} The one-rung bound is a shadow of a general statement:
the number of hypotheses in a correlation cap that an $L$-Lipschitz statistic can pairwise separate
by $\delta$ should be at most $1 + L\sqrt{2\varepsilon}/\delta$, independently of the dimension,
even though the cap contains exponentially many $\delta/L$-separated directions. This would exhibit
a strict quantitative separation between metric packing capacity and statistical resolving capacity.

\emph{Further programme.} Extend the ceiling to randomised statistics, converting the separation
bound into a bound on total-variation distance between decision distributions and hence a genuine
power bound; treat multi-response caps, where hypotheses live in an intersection of caps; determine
the optimal cut for threshold rules (for (R), is the midpoint $t = 0.554$ the most robust?); and
develop confidence intervals for the alignment so that the ceiling itself becomes an inferential
object.

\section{Conclusion}

Two hypotheses realised by predictor directions correlated at $1-\varepsilon$ sit inside a spherical
cap of chordal radius $\sqrt{2\varepsilon}$, and every statistic $L$-Lipschitz on the sphere is
thereby prevented from separating them by more than $L\sqrt{2\varepsilon}$ --- a bound containing no
sample size and exactly invariant under replication. It is sharp, attained by a distance statistic
and, at $L=1$, by an ordinary correlation against the contrast direction, which is therefore the
optimal smooth test.

For the recorded near-threshold instance the cap has angular radius $0.81^\circ$, the unit-sensitivity
ceiling is $0.0141$, full separation would require sensitivity at least $70$, the alignment is pinned
in $[0.9999,\,0.999968]$, the optimal smooth test improves on the recorded one by at most a factor
$1.7678$, and the cap has capacity for exactly one resolvable rung --- while a discontinuous
threshold statistic separates the identical configuration completely, at the price of being Lipschitz
for no constant. The practical upshot is a change of question: not ``how many more runs?'', for
which no number suffices, but ``is our statistic geometrically capable of the verdict we want, and
are we prepared to pay in brittleness for one that is?''

